.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.Dd July 31, 2026
.Dt LIBLOGCMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm logcmp_client_open ,
.Nm logcmp_logger_create ,
.Nm logcmp_emit ,
.Nm logcmp_flush ,
.Nm logcmp_stats ,
.Nm logcmp_query_next ,
.Nm logcmp_logger_destroy ,
.Nm logcmp_client_close
.Nd structured privacy-aware logging client
.Sh LIBRARY
.Li liblogcmp
.Pq Fl llogcmp
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In logcmp.h
.Ft int
.Fn logcmp_client_open "struct logcmp_client **client"
.Ft int
.Fo logcmp_logger_create
.Fa "struct logcmp_client *client"
.Fa "const char *subsystem"
.Fa "const char *category"
.Fa "struct logcmp_logger **logger"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fn logcmp_emit "struct logcmp_logger *logger" "const struct logcmp_emit_options *options"
.Ft int
.Fn logcmp_flush "struct logcmp_client *client"
.Ft int
.Fn logcmp_stats "struct logcmp_client *client" "struct logcmp_stats *stats"
.Ft int
.Fo logcmp_query_next
.Fa "struct logcmp_client *client"
.Fa "uint32_t minimum_severity"
.Fa "struct logcmp_query_cursor *cursor"
.Fa "void *record"
.Fa "size_t capacity"
.Fa "size_t *record_length"
.Fc
.Ft void
.Fn logcmp_logger_destroy "struct logcmp_logger *logger"
.Ft void
.Fn logcmp_client_close "struct logcmp_client *client"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
library implements version 5 of the global
.Dq system.Log
service.
Applications use this typed API; the library performs serviced discovery,
session negotiation, shared-memory attachment, framing, and descriptor
ownership internally.
Multiple opens in one process share one reference-counted client and one
independent provider session.
.Pp
Opening a client allocates no shared-memory ring.
Low-volume records travel inline over the service channel.
After sustained traffic the library lazily attaches a 16 KiB compact SPSC
ring.
If that ring fills, committed records are flushed, the compact ring is
detached, and a provider-negotiated bulk SPSC ring is attached before the
uncommitted record is retried.
Shape selection and watermarks are library policy and are not application
tuning knobs.
.Pp
.Fn logcmp_logger_create
creates an immutable logger handle for a subsystem and category.
The subsystem should be a stable reverse-domain identifier and the category
should identify a functional area.
The logger retains the client, so the caller may close its original client
reference after creating the logger.
.Pp
.Fn logcmp_emit
copies one bounded record into the process transport.
The options structure specifies a normalized severity from 1 through 24,
record kind, public, private, or private-hash message classification,
optional event, activity, signpost, trace, and span identifiers, and up to
.Dv LOGCMP_MAX_ATTRIBUTES
typed attributes.
Private fields are replaced by a fixed marker before retention.
Private-hash fields are replaced by a keyed 128-bit digest which is stable
only within the current LogCmp storage-manager epoch.
The random key is not persisted, so private hashes deliberately change after
a provider restart and are unsuitable as durable identifiers.
Attribute keys beginning with an underscore are reserved for authenticated
provider metadata.
Duplicate keys and malformed or oversized values are rejected.
Records whose privacy representation would exceed the negotiated record limit
are rejected with
.Er EMSGSIZE
before publication.
Every string and value is copied before the function returns.
.Pp
Normal publication performs no per-record control RPC.
Ring saturation returns
.Er EAGAIN
and increments the client drop counter.
After capacity recovers, the next publication first emits one public synthetic
event with subsystem
.Ql system.Log ,
category
.Ql delivery ,
event name
.Ql records-dropped ,
and a typed unsigned
.Ql count
attribute.
Once a record is committed to a shared ring, failure of its coalesced wakeup
does not turn the publication into a retryable error.
The library abandons the damaged session and reports success for that record;
the provider may already have consumed it or may consume it through its
fallback drain.
Use
.Fn logcmp_flush
when a caller requires a reported durability outcome.
The pending count is cleared only after that event is accepted; the cumulative
and per-severity statistics are never reset.
.Fn logcmp_flush
is an explicit synchronous durability boundary: it waits until the provider
has consumed records published before the request, submitted their redacted
representations to syslog, drained its bounded internal storage ring, and
synchronized the LogCmp store.
It does not claim that syslogd has committed
its separate copy to stable storage.
.Fn logcmp_stats
returns provider accepted and rejected counts, the local drop count, and the
last accepted sequence.
The statistics also contain 24 per-severity client drop counters indexed by
the normalized severity number minus one.
Provider-filtered and provider-rate-limited records are reported separately;
rate-limited counts also have 24 per-severity buckets.
Both deliberate drop
classes advance the provider's last-sequence value without being written to
the retained store or syslog projection.
.Pp
.Fn logcmp_query_next
returns the next retained, privacy-redacted record for the caller's immutable
serviced identity.
It returns 1 with one validated record, 0 at the current end, and -1 on error.
The caller initializes the cursor to zero and preserves it between calls.
A nonzero minimum severity filters lower-severity records.
The provider performs long searches in bounded slices and services other
storage requests between slices; this internal continuation is transparent to
the caller and remains subject to one total call deadline.
An
.Er EMSGSIZE
failure does not advance the cursor, so the call may be retried with a larger
buffer.
There is deliberately no API for selecting another service identity or for
turning an ordinary session into an administrative all-system reader.
.Pp
If the provider dies, the operation that observes the failure returns the
transport error because its acceptance is unknown.
The next operation reconnects the process-wide client and creates a fresh
ring when necessary.
The library never replays an ambiguously accepted record, so reconnect cannot
silently duplicate diagnostics.
.Pp
.Fn logcmp_logger_destroy
releases a logger's retained client reference.
The final
.Fn logcmp_client_close
requests a bounded drain, detaches the ring, and closes the service session,
so a later open creates a fresh producer sequence.
This is best-effort cleanup because the close API cannot report an error;
call
.Fn logcmp_flush
and check its result before close when delivery is required.
.Sh THREAD AND FORK SAFETY
Calls through different or shared logger handles are serialized safely within
one process.
Client and logger handles are not valid in a child after
.Xr fork 2 ;
the child must open a new client.
Destroying a handle concurrently with a call using that same handle is not
permitted.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libservice 3 ,
.Xr libshmring 3 ,
.Xr logd 8 ,
.Xr syslogd 8
.Sh SECURITY CONSIDERATIONS
The service label, provider instance, receive time, and provider sequence are
trusted metadata obtained from serviced or generated by the provider.
Clients cannot supply reserved fields or sink routing.
Private values are not rendered by the ordinary syslog projection.
The retained query contains only the same privacy-redacted representation and
is scoped by the authenticated serviced label, not by a caller-supplied name.
Application logging is not an audit trail; security decisions continue to use
OpenBSM.
